.lua
book.chapter(2, "Building MetaML and working at the prompt")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Build the executable you will actually use"
.pp
The core C++ interpreter is header-only, but a command-line executable gives us
a repeatable environment for learning. The current dependency requires C++20:
DSLtk.hpp uses concepts and class-valued template parameters. Older references
to a C++17 MetaML interface do not override that requirement. CMake configures
the project, a C++ compiler builds it, and Python runs the repository's checking
scripts. A D compiler is useful when checking dialect parity but is unnecessary
for a C++-only first session.
.lua
book.code("sh", [=[# Run from the repository's DSLtk directory.
cmake -S . -B build -DMETAML_BUILD_REPL=ON -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF
cmake --build build --target metaml-repl-cpp --parallel 2
build/metaml-repl-cpp --version
build/metaml-repl-cpp --stdlib metalib -e 'List.sum [1, 2, 3]']=],
    "Configure, compile, and run")
.endlua
.pp
The target selects metaml-cli/metaml.cpp in this checkout. Some older prose
uses a repl directory; follow the actual CMake source path when investigating
build failures. The executable detects the portable host-library bundle and
installs it. It also loads the portable prelude unless instructed otherwise.
This is why a CLI example can use Graph or ASTBuilder whereas an untouched
embedding Session initially has only its core facilities.
.pp
Building an interpreter and building this book are separate tasks. The book's
build.sh can compile luaROFF, but it does not silently build MetaML. Supply an
existing MetaML executable when requesting checked book examples. This keeps
the publishing workflow useful to readers who only want a PDF, and keeps a
language build failure distinguishable from a typesetting failure.
.sh 2 "Three ways to submit a program"
.pp
Interactive input is convenient for discovering a type or testing a small
expression. A terminal shows the historical prompt spelling meaml>, while
piped input suppresses prompts. An unfinished construct requests continuation
instead of immediately treating every newline as a complete program. A file
is better for a multi-definition experiment because it preserves source
locations and can be rerun after changes.
.lua
book.code("sh", [=[build/metaml-repl-cpp --stdlib metalib
build/metaml-repl-cpp --stdlib metalib program.ml
build/metaml-repl-cpp --no-tty --no-history --stdlib metalib \
  -e 'Test.equal "arithmetic" (6 * 7) 42'
build/metaml-repl-cpp --dump-ast program.ml]=], "Interactive, file, and expression modes")
.endlua
.pp
Files are quiet except for explicit output and diagnostics; the -e route uses
the REPL's formatting of results. Do not write a test that assumes those two
display modes are interchangeable. For a durable example, use Test.equal or
Test.check and examine the exit status. For data exchange, use an explicit
serialization routine or the embedding API rather than scraping a prompt.
.sh 2 "Ask focused questions at the prompt"
.pp
The directives form a small workbench. #type checks an expression without
evaluating it. #ast shows syntax. #trace evaluates with a reduction trace.
#stack exposes the interpreter's observable result stack. #load remembers and
executes a file, #reload executes the remembered file again, and #clear resets
the interactive state according to the language policy. #help and #quit belong
to the generic shell. Directives are top-level commands, not ordinary functions
that can be inserted into a lambda body.
.lua
book.code("text", [=[meaml> increment x := x + 1
meaml> #type increment
meaml> #trace increment 41
meaml> #load "experiment.ml"
meaml> #reload]=], "A workbench transcript: enter the commands, not the prompts")
.endlua
.pp
Use #type before tracing when a composition is ambiguous. If the type is wrong,
an execution trace cannot repair the interface. Conversely, successful type
checking does not prove that a match is exhaustive, a divisor is nonzero, or a
host capability has been granted. Choose the observation appropriate to the
stage you are investigating. The diagnostics chapter gives a code-family map
for making this choice systematic.
.sh 2 "History, reload, and reproducibility"
.pp
History normally lives below XDG_STATE_HOME/metaml or the user's local state
directory. Readline integration is optional in the C++ wrapper. Automated
examples disable history and terminal behavior so tests do not depend on a
user's interactive environment. The wrapper checks the last loaded file for
changes when it processes later input; it is not a continuously running
background watcher. A file edited while the prompt is idle is not evidence
that a separate evaluation has already occurred.
.pp
Reloading is execution. Earlier definitions may be replaced by later bindings,
and output or host mutations can happen again. Imports have their own once-only
cache and are not identical to #load. A robust development loop keeps reusable
modules mostly pure, places effects in explicit functions, and invokes those
functions intentionally. This also makes tests easier to isolate in fresh
processes, which is how the book's complete listings are verified.
.pp
Select the standard-library directory explicitly when reproducibility matters.
Environment-based discovery is useful for an installed application, but an
unexpected METAML_STDLIB setting can select a different tree during development.
Record both the interpreter version and the library revision when reporting a
problem. They form a working pair: new source wrappers can depend on primitives
that an older executable has not installed.
.sh 2 "From experiment to regression"
.pp
A good interactive discovery becomes a small script with a stated invariant.
When testing an error, keep the failing submission separate from the successful
one that verifies recovery. When testing output, capture stdout and stderr
independently. When testing a transformation, include a normal input, a boundary
input, and an input whose shape challenges your assumption. This costs little
for an interpreter and prevents examples from succeeding merely because an old
session happened to contain a useful binding.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[twice x := x + x
Test.equal "normal input" (twice 21) 42
Test.equal "zero boundary" (twice 0) 0
Test.equal "negative input" (twice (~3)) (~6)]=], "Turn a prompt experiment into a script")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read CMakeLists.txt and metaml-cli/metaml.cpp for supported targets and options;
metaml-cli/metaml.d is the mirrored native entry point. The generic REPL is
inside DSLtk.hpp, and MetaML's ReplLanguage connects it to evaluation. Build
with testing enabled when you want ctest coverage. As an exercise, save the
last listing to a file, run it in file and expression modes, and explain which
parts of the output are program effects and which are shell presentation.
